          "KE = ½ m v²",
          "PE = m g h"
        ],
        [
          "Real-World Example",
          "A moving bullet or flowing river water",
          "Water stored behind a dam or a stretched bow string"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "48fasn3"
    },
    {
      "id": "9s6n4qb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Law of Conservation of Energy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "63iltrf",
      "content": "The law of conservation of energy states that energy can neither be created nor destroyed; it can only be converted from one form to another."
    },
    {
      "id": "ma5lpzf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Mechanical Energy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5xa5onk",
      "content": "Mechanical energy is the sum of kinetic energy and potential energy."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8i688xn",
      "content": "Mechanical Energy = KE + PE"
    },
    {
      "id": "g3v0ofy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Meaning of Power"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jzc8fhi",
      "content": "Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ohvy57b",
      "content": "Formula: Power = Work / Time"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dgdkunn",
      "content": "SI Unit of Power = Watt (W)"
    },
    {
      "id": "okkgflj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Units of Power"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "1 Horsepower (HP) equals 746 Watts, a frequently asked unit conversion in exams.",
        "1 Kilowatt-hour (kWh) is equal to 3.6 × 10⁶ Joules, which is the standard commercial unit of electrical energy.",
        "If the velocity of an object is doubled, its Kinetic Energy becomes four times its original value."
      ],
      "id": "zp3tj9k"
    },
